\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, all experiments in this section use the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; full templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}).

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_count_curves.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_bcs_scaling.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\caption{\textbf{(a)} Count-degradation curves across four datasets. NER recall generally declines and RE accuracy (both FewRel and DocRED) declines monotonically with schema size; MAVEN relaxed F1 increases (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). \textbf{(b)} BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. The BCS--accuracy slope is consistent across the independent 16-relation pool and within-pool 16-relation subsets (permutation $p = 0.60$); the wider BCS range available at 80 relations produces larger absolute gaps.}
\label{fig:main_results}
\end{figure}

We first examine how extraction performance changes as the number of types in the prompt schema increases (Experiment~1; Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a).

\paragraph{NER (Few-NERD).}
Recall declines from 0.72 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.42 at count${}\,=\,64$ ($-$42\% relative), with minor non-monotonicity within sampling variability (bootstrap CIs overlap). Precision rises inversely (0.11 $\to$ 0.52), reflecting a shift from over-prediction to under-prediction as candidate types increase.

\paragraph{RE (FewRel).}
Accuracy drops monotonically from 85.7\% at count${}\,=\,1$ through 58.8\% at count${}\,=\,40$ to 50.4\% at count${}\,=\,80$, a near-halving when the model must discriminate among all 80 relation types simultaneously. (The count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation uses 40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool, not an independent schema.)

\paragraph{Document-level RE (DocRED).}
Accuracy declines from 88.0\% (count 1) to 38.7\% (count 96), a 56\% relative drop consistent with FewRel, confirming generalization to document-level RE.

\paragraph{ED (MAVEN).}
MAVEN relaxed F1 \emph{increases} from 0.106 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.247 at count${}\,=\,44$---a reversal explained by relaxed matching, same-domain hierarchy providing disambiguating context, and class imbalance where rare types benefit from co-presentation with related types (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}).

\paragraph{Cross-model replication.}
Channel~1 replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: $-$43\,pp recall decline on Few-NERD (vs.\ $-$34\,pp for Qwen3-32B; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}). Per-batch correlation $r = 0.725$, indicating schema configuration drives difficulty rather than model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Summary.}
Schema size degrades NER and RE (Channel~1), but MAVEN's reversed trend shows task structure mediates the relationship. DocRED confirms generalization to document-level RE (96 types). Both channels replicate on Llama-3.1-8B (count $r = 0.725$; confusability in \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate---the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$---and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & $7{\times}10^{-7}$ \\
FewRel & Qwen3 & 3{,}160 & 0.187 & 0.035 & {[}0.145, 0.231{]} & $3{\times}10^{-26}$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & $7{\times}10^{-42}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. $r^2$ indicates the fraction of variance in type confusion explained by embedding similarity. All three correlations are modest in magnitude but robust across datasets and embedding models (all $p \ll 0.001$). FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per type) eliminates frequency as a confound on its pairwise signal. Confusion rate for each ordered pair $(t_i, t_j)$ is computed as the total bidirectional confusion count $C(t_i{\to}t_j) + C(t_j{\to}t_i)$ divided by the sum of gold frequencies, aggregated over all count conditions; CIs are pair-level percentile bootstrap ($B{=}1{,}000$).}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

The correlations are statistically significant but modest: $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}3.5\%$. Type-level cluster bootstrap yields wider but still significant CIs (Few-NERD: [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN: [0.081, 0.185]; $B = 10{,}000$). All three datasets show significant positive correlations between pairwise SCS and type confusion rates. Spearman correlations corroborate: $\rho = 0.144\text{--}0.227$ across all pairs, strengthening to $\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$ among pairs with nonzero confusion (though conditioning on the dependent variable may inflate this).

The remaining ${\sim}$97--99\% of variance likely reflects entity frequency, prompt position effects \cite{lost-in-middle-2024}, mention ambiguity, and type definition quality. We frame pairwise SCS as one specific, actionable mechanism---semantic overlap between type labels is directly amenable to schema design---rather than the totality of degradation. Although pairwise $r^2$ is modest, the signal compounds at the batch level into quartile gaps of 3.9--5.3\,pp under typical batching, reaching 31.2\,pp only under extreme-batch construction ($-$25.5\,pp under v2 prompts; typical-batch gap ${\approx}\,2\text{--}4$\,pp, \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}).

\subsection{BCS-Controlled Experiments}
\label{sec:bcs_controlled}

We fix the type count and vary BCS to obtain controlled stratified comparison evidence. Our design controls for count and pool composition. However, BCS-defined strata may differ in type frequency, definition quality, or prompt position---confounds we cannot fully rule out. FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per type) inherently eliminates type frequency as a confound. Table~\ref{tab:bcs_controlled} reports FewRel accuracy under low-BCS and high-BCS batches; the within-pool analysis (\S\ref{sec:within_pool}) controls for residual confounds from pool composition.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Schema} & \textbf{Count} & \textbf{Low-BCS} & \textbf{High-BCS} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
16-rel & 8 & 88.9\% & 79.8\% & $-$9.1 & --- \\
80-rel & 8 & 82.3\% & 51.0\% & $-$31.2 & {[}17.7, 42.7{]} \\
80-rel & 16 & 82.8\% & 51.6\% & $-$31.2 & {[}22.9, 39.6{]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
