For representative batches from the 80-relation pool ($n{=}100$ random 16-type subsets), the typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI: [0.4, 4.0])---roughly 7\% of the 31.2\,pp extreme-batch gap. Quartile comparison confirms: bottom-quartile BCS batches average 82.8\% vs.\ 78.9\% for top-quartile ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp). The 31.2\,pp figure reflects maximum BCS contrast, not the typical batch experience. Pair-level $r^2{=}1.5\%$ aggregates to batch-level $R^2{=}22$--$53\%$ across datasets, a 14--35$\times$ amplification consistent with averaging over $\sim\binom{k}{2}$ correlated pair effects per batch.

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
The effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: BCS gaps of $-$27.1\,pp (count 8) and $-$23.9\,pp (count 16), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's gap. Both models converge to $\approx$51\% under high confusability regardless of count, while divergence is confined to the low-BCS regime---suggesting a task-inherent difficulty floor under high confusability (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}).

\paragraph{Prompt sensitivity.}
Under the v2 prompt (stricter format constraints), the extreme-batch BCS gap is $-$25.5\,pp (low-BCS 85.2\% vs.\ high-BCS 59.7\%; 95\% CI: [22.4, 28.6]; $n{=}25$; $r = -0.919$; $p < 0.0001$). Compared to v1's $-$31.2\,pp, the effect attenuates modestly ($-$5.7\,pp) but remains highly significant. Part of this attenuation reflects the narrower BCS range available under our v2 balanced sampling (0.36--0.80 vs.\ 0.47--0.97); however, the stronger per-pair correlation ($r = -0.919$) under v2 suggests that per-unit sensitivity to confusability is at least as large when frequency and sample-size confounds are controlled. NER v2 yields consistent batch-level correlations ($r = +0.58$ vs.\ $r = +0.50$ under v1). Prompt design modulates the magnitude but does not eliminate the confusability effect (full analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_error_taxonomy.pdf}
\caption{Error taxonomy decomposition. IntraSub scales monotonically with BCS in both tasks. RE missed rates remain flat (10--16\%), but NER missed rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic.}
\label{fig:error_taxonomy}
\end{figure}

Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$) for NER and $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 10^{-6}$) for RE. Under closed-set classification, non-\texttt{none} errors are necessarily intra-batch; the more informative finding is that IntraSub confusion frequency correlates with pairwise SCS ($\rho{=}0.954$, $p{<}0.001$). In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic, indicating confusable types impair entity detection itself. Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches.

These results establish two channels: \textbf{Channel~1} (count $\to$ recall decline) and \textbf{Channel~2} (confusability $\to$ type confusion), cleanly separable in RE but coupled in NER where type ambiguity and boundary ambiguity interact.

\subsection{Centrality Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}

Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--F1 correlation (Qwen3 $\rho = +0.184$, $p = 0.025$; MiniLM $\rho = +0.208$, $p = 0.011$). This traces to same-domain embedding collapse \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}: MAVEN's FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly, so mean BCS conflates confusability with centrality---high-BCS types are semantically central and easy to extract. Controlling for log frequency and degree centrality, the positive correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}). The pairwise SCS signal remains intact ($r = 0.129$, $p < 10^{-10}$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}); the problem is per-type aggregation, not the underlying relationship. Few-NERD's cross-domain schema does not exhibit this confound ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$). Mean BCS is thus reliable only for cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas require pairwise SCS or centrality adjustment.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate how prompt schema size and inter-type confusability affect zero-shot LLM extraction across three information extraction tasks and four datasets: named entity recognition (NER), sentence-level relation extraction (RE), document-level RE, and event detection (ED).

\subsection{Tasks and Datasets}
\label{sec:datasets}

\paragraph{Few-NERD (NER).}
Few-NERD \cite{fewnerd-2021} provides 66 fine-grained entity types organized into 8 coarse categories (person, location, organization, building, art, product, event, other). The dataset spans diverse domains, making it suitable for cross-domain evaluation. We sample 1{,}000 test instances and evaluate with token-level text matching.

\paragraph{FewRel (RE).}
FewRel \cite{fewrel-2018} contains 80 relation types sourced from Wikidata. Since the validation split covers only 16 relations, we combine the training and validation splits to obtain the full 80-relation pool required for schema-scale experiments, and sample 1{,}000 balanced instances (12--13 per relation type, stratified to ensure equal representation). Each instance provides a sentence with marked head and tail entities; the task is to classify the relation.

\paragraph{DocRED (Document-level RE).}
DocRED \cite{docred-2019} contains 96 relation types sourced from Wikidata, evaluated at the document level where entity pairs may span multiple sentences. We sample balanced instances and formulate the task identically to FewRel: given a document with marked head and tail entities, classify the relation. DocRED extends the RE evaluation to a larger schema (96 vs.\ 80 types) and document-level context, testing whether confusability effects persist under longer input contexts and cross-sentence reasoning.

\paragraph{MAVEN (ED).}
MAVEN \cite{maven-2020} defines 168 event types derived from the FrameNet ontology, making it a same-domain schema. We use the validation set (8{,}042 sentences containing 18{,}904 event mentions) for evaluation, as test set labels are not publicly available. The task requires both trigger identification and event type classification.

Table~\ref{tab:datasets} summarizes the dataset statistics, including the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) ranges defined in \S\ref{sec:scs}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrlll}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Task} & \textbf{\# Types} & \textbf{\# Eval Units} & \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{BCS Range} & \textbf{Embedding} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & NER & 66 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.77--0.93 & Qwen3-32B \\
FewRel & RE (sent.) & 80 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.42--0.97 & MiniLM \\
